      en: "A fascinating basilica from 1906, built of mud bricks featuring wall paintings that blend local and western art. Combines local and European building styles. Founded by French missionaries. Expanded in 1920. Is an official Minor Basilica. Thus, the site is among the characteristic points of Ghana — a country shaped by its Atlantic coastline, the Volta river system, the legacy of the Ashanti Kingdom, and its cocoa and gold wealth — and offers insight into the regional identity. Located in the West African state of Ghana along the Gulf of Guinea coast."
